"""Layer-0 MLP down-projection component 3742: a number-word detector.

The component reads the current token and writes "a number or quantity word is here"
into the residual stream. How strongly it responds depends on how large a quantity the
word names:

- big round magnitudes are strongest: hundred, 百 and 千 (and 百分之, 百年), thousand,
  century, cent;
- medium: million, billion, trillion, percent, decade, dozen, the tens (twenty..ninety);
- small: the teens (ten..nineteen) and one, three..nine; "two" is weakest;
- time-span and unit words (year, years) are weak;
- days, months, hours, seconds, ordinals ("first", "second") and words like "many" or
  "some" stay near zero.

Digits, punctuation and ordinary words also stay near zero. Two more cases fire: the word
"of" directly after a plural magnitude ("thousands of", "hundreds of"), and the
end-of-document token. Matching ignores case, a leading space and a leading dash.
"""

READS = ()

ONES = {"one": 0.7, "two": 0.3, "three": 0.7, "four": 0.8, "five": 0.8,
        "six": 0.9, "seven": 0.9, "eight": 0.9, "nine": 0.95, "zero": 0.7}
TEENS = {"ten": 1.5, "eleven": 1.4, "twelve": 1.4, "thirteen": 1.4, "fourteen": 1.4,
         "fifteen": 1.5, "sixteen": 1.4, "seventeen": 1.4, "eighteen": 1.6,
         "nineteen": 1.7, "teens": 0.3, "nineteenth": 1.1, "twentieth": 1.2}
