A fragment beginning e/i/j/y tends positive; one beginning o/r/s/z tends
negative. Bare apostrophes and Cyrillic palatalizing letters are positive,
while common English suffixes have weak responses. A few letter sequences,
fragment length and case refine the estimate. Whitespace suppresses
the interaction. This deliberately omits word-specific and wider-context
variation; it uses spelling classes, not a token vocabulary.
The previous fragment is forgotten at each document boundary.
